## Answers & Rationale

**Q1 — B.** Subagent delegation earns its overhead (fresh context, independent reasoning) only for work needing isolation, multi-step exploration, or parallelism. A single deterministic tool call gets none of those benefits, so routing it through `Task` just adds latency for nothing. *(A: hooks are for deterministic enforcement, not judgment calls about "is this simple enough." C: model tier doesn't remove the structural round-trip overhead. D: resources are for read-only data, not write actions like `reset_password`.)*

**Q2 — B.** Nullable fields let the schema represent a legitimately absent value truthfully instead of forcing fabrication or failure. *(A/D: `tool_choice` variants don't touch what happens to an individual required field. C: more few-shot examples don't create a valid "absent" representation.)*

**Q3 — B.** Splitting an overloaded, polymorphic tool into purpose-specific tools removes the ambiguity that let a destructive action hide inside a parameter value. *(A: better prose is a band-aid on a structurally ambiguous tool. C: `isError` only helps after the damage is done. D: soft sequencing instructions aren't deterministic and don't fix the same-call ambiguity.)*

**Q4 — B.** `CLAUDE.md` instructions are advisory natural language; an unattended run has no one to give "confirmation" to, so a `PreToolUse` hook is needed as a deterministic backstop. *(A: relocating the same advisory text changes nothing about enforceability. C: `argument-hint` is for interactive CLI use, not automation. D: excluding `git push` entirely is too blunt — it blocks legitimate pushes too.)*

**Q5 — B.** Explicitly provided case-facts should out-rank an arbitrary retrieved source; conflicts should be flagged, not silently overwritten. *(A: throws out a useful tool entirely. C: context size isn't the issue — precedence is. D: shortening the new info doesn't change its epistemic weight.)*

**Q6 — A.** Needs both: the schema communicates the expected format (reducing malformed output), and the hook deterministically catches whatever slips through anyway. *(B: neither lever guarantees compliance. C: schema alone still allows malformed dates through since nothing enforces it. D: hook alone works but forces excessive retries since the model gets no format signal upfront.)*

**Q7 — A and D (you answered B and D — partial, scored incorrect).** Toolset scoping (A) removes the capability from junior sessions entirely; a role-check hook (D) is the deterministic backstop if scoping drifts. *(B — your pick — is a real principle, purpose-specific tools, but doesn't fix who has access to the tool at all, which is the actual defect. C: louder prompt wording is the same failed mechanism, just emphasized. E: refunds have side effects and can't be a read-only resource.)*

**Q8 — A.** Directory-scoped config loads specifically when work happens in that directory, rather than competing for attention as one paragraph in a repo-wide file. *(B: same location, just louder wording, doesn't fix the scoping problem. C: a skill the model must remember to invoke reintroduces the same reliability gap. D: bigger context window doesn't fix a scoping/organization defect.)*

**Q9 — B.** A vague label produces inconsistent judgment calls; an operational, testable rubric gives the same concrete criteria for every input. *(A: temperature 0 only removes randomness for identical inputs, not inconsistency across different-but-similar ones. C: few-shot examples are an indirect, incomplete substitute for an explicit rubric. D: majority voting just averages several inconsistent judgments of the same vague rubric.)*

**Q10 — A.** Trust from validation is trust in a specific population; a distribution shift (new claim type) should trigger renewed/stratified sampling, not be caught only by luck. *(B: permanent max sampling defeats the point of automating at all. C: a global confidence change is the wrong scope — the issue was localized. D: cross-domain trap — patching the prompt doesn't fix the missing drift-detection/monitoring layer that would catch the *next* shift too.)*

**Q11 — B.** The API is stateless; the full history, including the exact `tool_use` block, must be resent so the `tool_result`'s `tool_use_id` can correlate back to it. *(A: false — the API doesn't remember prior turns server-side. C: replacing the block with a summary breaks the required correlation. D: `stop_reason` is server-set, not client-set, and tool results go in a user-role message, not a system message.)*

**Q12 — A.** `is_error: true` is structured metadata that removes the need for the model to parse free text to detect failure. *(B: still relies on fragile text-prefix parsing, just documented instead of fixed. C: actively dangerous — bakes in false success reporting. D: soft keyword-scanning instruction is unreliable under load.)*

**Q13 — A.** Compaction condenses resolved work into a summary, keeping useful decisions/conventions while dropping the noisy detail that was contaminating new tasks — without discarding everything. *(B: discards the useful conventions along with the noise. C: `context: fork` is for subagent isolation, not a single ongoing session's own accumulation. D: bigger context window doesn't stop stale-but-present context from wrongly influencing new work.)*

**Q14 — A.** Independent calls should be batched into one turn's multiple `tool_use` blocks and executed in parallel by the harness, instead of serialized needlessly. *(B: subagent delegation adds context-loading overhead for zero benefit over plain parallel calls to the same tool. C: temperature doesn't affect turn-taking structure. D: "strictly better in every case" is the tell — a batched custom tool loses per-call error granularity and isn't universally superior.)*

**Q15 — A.** Static, pre-existing reference content fits a resource; a computed, action-driven result fits a tool. *(B: overgeneralizes — not all data-returning capabilities are resources, especially computed ones. C: backwards, and the stated reasoning about parameters is also factually wrong — resource templates can take parameters. D: contradicts the entire point of the resources-vs-tools distinction being a tested concept.)*

